Sometime around middle school, I found a monster in mind.

I don’t know if he was always there. But he was here now. I tried to ignore him, but he just enjoyed hide and seek.

We took turns. I hid. He seeked. He hid. I could not find him.

He was slippery! I couldn’t see his face, but I knew he was there. He hung in the fabric of my now ill-fitting shirts. Waved to me at the edges of childhood innocence.

I wasn’t a monster at first. Childhood and adolescense is a swirl of growth and experience. Growth brings awareness. Of yourself. Of others. How others see you.

I don’t know when, but soon he was with me every moment. He was in the pointed "laughter" of friends. I saw him in the eyes of people I respected, trusted. Loved. 

He made me smell bad. He made me ugly. He made my natural body bloated and distended.

He made himself visible to others through my embarassement. He did not enjoy my shame, but he knew it was right.

He was a monster. Even I looked at him the same way. Why wouldn't the world see me as a monster too?

So it was me and the monster then. I let him in. He whispered things to me that I didn’t understand. He didn’t mean too, but he hurt me. We were alone.

My body continued to evolve. I ate less. Dranks cups and cups of coffee to quell hunger. I was proud of my discipline in taming the monster's. Clothes fit more correctly. People smiled at me. I laughed with friends again.

But it was still just me and the monster. It became a familiar ritual, looking at our forms in the mirror. Maybe some objective part of my brain could see my slimness. I was never thin. But I was healthy then, eating habits aside. I look back now and I see a healthy 7th grader.

That isn’t what I remember, though. 

Fat fat fat fat fat fat fat fat fat

Me and the monster would stutter in the mirror. Look at the way your skin hangs. Look at the paleness of your skin, disturbed by rosy blotches. A whale amongst men.

I tolerated the monster, and so the world tolerated me. I was grateful for the friends and experiences that I had. How often do monsters get to have friends, family, love, excitement? 

Gratitude was the only option. Gratitude and the conflicted understanding that other people might see themselves as monsters too. I wanted to make friends with other "monsters". I didn't see them as monsters. They were beautiful. Kind. Funny. Unique. I wanted them to see that. To feel less alone. Like I wanted.

I was the monster. Always tolerated.

We became close. Even as I lost myself in the shadow of the monster, it felt right. I was a monster. We were one.

I lost interests. My work ethic plummeted. Friends and loved ones grew distant. But somewhere along the way I learned that the world only tolerates monsters to an extent.

